questionText:
Frequency of the de-Broglie wave of electron in Bohr's first orbit of hydrogen atom is _______ \(\times 10^{13} \mathrm{~Hz}\) (nearest integer).
\(\begin{aligned}
& \text { [Given : } \mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{H}}(\text { Rydberg constant })=2.18 \times 10^{-18} \mathrm{~J}, h \text { (Plank's } \\
& \text { constant })=6.6 \times 10^{-34} \mathrm{~J} \text {.s.] }
\end{aligned}\)

solution:
According to de-Broglie wavelength equation,

$$

\lambda=\frac{h}{m v} \Rightarrow \lambda \propto \frac{1}{v}

$$

According to photoelectric effect,

$$

\begin{array}{l}

\mathrm{hv}-\mathrm{hv}_{0}=\frac{1}{2} \mathrm{mv}^{2} ; \mathrm{v}-\mathrm{v}_{0}=\frac{1}{2}=\frac{\mathrm{mv}^{2}}{\mathrm{~h}} \\

\mathrm{v}-\mathrm{v}_{0} \propto \mathrm{v}^{2} \\

\mathrm{v} \propto\left(\mathrm{v}-\mathrm{v}_{0}\right)^{1 / 2} \\

\therefore \quad \lambda \propto \frac{1}{\left(\mathrm{v}-\mathrm{v}_{0}\right)^{1 / 2}}

\end{array}

$$

$\mathrm{E}=\mathrm{hv}$
$$
\begin{aligned}
&=6.63 \times 10^{-34} \times 2.47 \times 10^{15} \\
&=1.640 \times 10^{-18} \mathrm{~J}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
&\Delta E=-2.0 \times 10^{-18} \times\left(\frac{1}{2^2}-\frac{1}{1^2}\right) \\
&=-2.0 \times 10^{-18} \times \frac{-3}{4} \\
&=1.5 \times 10^{-18} \\
&\Delta E=\frac{h c}{\lambda} \\
&\lambda=\frac{h c}{\Delta E}=\frac{6.6 \times 10^{-34} \times 3 \times 10^8}{1.5 \times 10^{-18}} \\
&=1.325 \times 10^{-7} \mathrm{~m}
\end{aligned}
$$

$\bar{v}=\frac{1}{\lambda}=R_H Z\left(\frac{1}{n_1^2}-\frac{1}{n_2^2}\right)$
In Balmer series $n_1=2 \& n_2=3,4,5 \ldots$... Last line of the spectrum is called series limit.
Limiting line is the line of shortest wavelength and high energy when $n_2=\infty$
$$
\begin{aligned}
\therefore \quad \bar{v}= & \frac{1}{\lambda}=\frac{R_H}{n_1^2}=\frac{3.29 \times 10^{15}}{2^2}=\frac{3.29 \times 10^{15}}{4} \\
& =8.22 \times 10^{14} \mathrm{~s}^{-1}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\lambda=\frac{\mathrm{h}}{\mathrm{mv}}=\frac{6.63 \times 10^{-34}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \times 10^3} \equiv 0.40 \mathrm{~nm}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \Delta \mathrm{x} . \mathrm{m} \Delta \mathrm{v}=\frac{\mathrm{h}}{4 \pi} \\
& \Delta \mathrm{x}=\frac{\mathrm{h}}{4 \pi \mathrm{m} \Delta \mathrm{v}} \\
& \Delta \mathrm{v}=600 \times \frac{0.005}{100}=0.03 \\
& \Rightarrow \Delta \mathrm{x}=\frac{6.625 \times 10^{-34}}{4 \times 3.14 \times 9.1 \times 10^{-31} \times 0.03}=1.92 \times 10^{-3} \mathrm{~m}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \Delta \mathrm{E}=\mathrm{E}_2-\mathrm{E}_1=-\frac{1.312 \times 10^6}{2^2}-\left(-\frac{1.312 \times 10^6}{1}\right) \\
& =9.84 \times 10^5 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \Delta \mathrm{x} . \Delta \mathrm{V} \geq \frac{\mathrm{h}}{4 \pi \mathrm{m}} \\
